<h2>TDEE calculator formula</h2><p>The first step is Mifflin-St Jeor resting energy expenditure: 10 × weight (kg) + 6.25 × height (cm) − 5 × age, plus 5 for the male equation or minus 161 for the female equation. The original study derived the equation from measured resting energy expenditure in healthy adults. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mifflin et al. study</a>.</p>
<h2>From REE to TDEE</h2><p>Total daily energy expenditure is estimated by multiplying REE by an activity factor. The factor is the least precise part of a simple TDEE calculator because real daily movement, occupation and training load vary continuously.</p>
<h2>Why TDEE is not a fixed personal number</h2><p>Energy expenditure changes with activity, body composition, temperature, illness, training and other factors. Treat the result as a starting estimate rather than a measurement.</p>
<h2>Health and nutrition limitation</h2><p>This tool does not prescribe a calorie deficit, weight-loss target or medical nutrition plan. People with medical, pregnancy, eating-disorder or performance-nutrition needs should use individualized professional guidance.</p>
<h2>Treat TDEE as a starting estimate, then calibrate</h2><p>Activity multipliers compress many behaviors into one category, so estimated TDEE can differ meaningfully from real energy expenditure. If body weight is stable over several weeks under consistent intake tracking, that observed intake can help calibrate the estimate. Changes in training volume, occupation, sleep, illness and body mass can change expenditure. For medical nutrition, eating disorders, pregnancy or other health conditions, use individualized professional guidance rather than a generic calculator.</p>
